cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this artifact, please cite it and the paper it accompanies once published. This artifact accompanies a prototype-stage manuscript that is not yet submitted (see README.md); cite with that context."
title: "DAIM-OS Self-Healing Artifact: Event-Driven Fault Detection and Autonomous Path Recovery over the Table-and-Signal Control Path"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Banjar
    given-names: Ameen
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0871-5153"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-08-11
license: Apache-2.0
# doi: TODO -- fill in once this repository is pushed to GitHub, the Zenodo
# GitHub integration is enabled for it, and a GitHub Release/tag is created
# to trigger Zenodo's automatic archival and DOI minting (same process used
# for the two artifacts referenced below). Do not fabricate a DOI here.
repository-code: "https://github.com/ameen-banjar/daim-os-self-healing-artifact"
abstract: >-
  Reproducibility artifact for "Self-Healing DAIM-OS: Event-Driven Fault
  Detection and Autonomous Path Recovery over the Table-and-Signal Control
  Path" (Paper 3 in the DAIM six-paper programme, prototype-stage, not yet
  submitted). Contains an event-driven self-healing agent
  (daim_link_agent.py) that subscribes to real OVSDB Interface.link_state
  notifications, recomputes an alternate path with breadth-first search over
  a declared topology graph, installs it through the existing DAIM-OS OVS
  adapter, and suppresses repeated repair processing on a flapping interface
  via an IDLE/ACTIVE/HELD-DOWN hold-down state machine; pure-logic unit
  tests for both the path computation and the hold-down state machine,
  runnable without Mininet/OVS; the network-level Stage 3 raw data (five
  autonomous link-failure repetitions, plus scripted non-autonomous
  switch/controller-failure conditions) and reports; and the figure-
  generation script and resulting figures used in the manuscript.
keywords:
  - software-defined networking
  - self-healing networks
  - event-driven fault detection
  - autonomous path recovery
  - OVSDB
  - OpenFlow
  - hold-down timers
  - control-plane resilience
  - reproducible systems research
